For nearly every game other than roguelikes, if you make it to
level 15 in 10 consequative games, you may as well start all
of your new games at level 15.  So it seems common for folks
to believe this principle applies to roguelikes as well.  Is it so
bad to save every 5 levels and to restore a few times?  It does
not seem so to players of other games.

> If you just wanted to get to know
> all the stages of the game, why didn't you just use explore mode?

It took me actually experiencing that this principle that applies
to other games does not apply to roguelikes.  I'd played freshman
soccer, moved up to junior varsity, moved up to varisity.  I'd seen
plenty of chess players move up through the ranks and get
rated into tougher sections in tournies.  I fully expected this
principle to apply to Nethack and it had to be hammered into
me the hard way that it doesn't.

> Just
> typing 'n' at the 'die?' prompt is considerably easier than backing up and
> restoring savefiles.

To me the problem with that is it didn't fit the model.  I wasn't
interested in avoiding this death to learn about this level.  I
expected to be able to bank my experience.

The trouble is nethack has so much context that there's no way
to start a game on level 10, or among the players rated 1200+,
in on the varsity team.  What works in essentially all other game
types doesn't work in Nethack.

But having had to learn that the hard way, I am dubious that telling
a new player that is going to help.



